    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d277">

                <pb facs="d276-2" n="2" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76ve10/pgd276-2"/>

                <head>277. Telegram 43343 From the Department of State to the Embassy in the
                    Dominican Republic<anchor n="1" xml:id="d277-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d277-f2"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1970-03-25T05:25:00+00:00">March 25, 1970,
                            0525Z</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <pb facs="d277-1" n="1" xml:id="pgd277-1"/>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subj:</label>
                    <item>Kidnapping U.S. Air Attache</item>
                </list>
                <list type="references">
                    <head>Ref:</head>
                    <item>SD–696</item>
                </list>
                <p>1. We share your deep concern and believe that point has now repeat now been
                    reached warranting your personal vigorous effort to persuade Balaguer to release
                    prisoners and take effective steps to obtain safe return of Col. Crowley.</p>
                <p>2. Accordingly, you are instructed to do so ASAP.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d277-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d277-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files 1970–73, POL 23–8 DOM REP.
                    Secret; Limdis; Flash. Drafted by Hurwitch and approved by Nelson Ledsky (S/S).
                    For telegram 696 from Santo Domingo, see <ref target="#d276">Document 276</ref>.
                    On March 25, the Ambassador reported in Telegram 700 from Santo Domingo that he
                    had gotten President Balaguer out of bed to ascertain “the intentions of the
                    GODR and what steps had been taken to secure Col. Crowley’s safety and eventual
                    release.” (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d277-f2" n="2" xml:id="d277-f2-content">
                <p>The Department of State concurred with Ambassador Meloy’s concerns and instructed
                    him to take “personal vigorous effort to persuade Balaguer to release prisoners
                    and take effective steps to obtain safe return of Col. Crowley.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
